@page "/counter"

<PageTitle>Counter · BevTools</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <p class="bb-overline">Scenario · Rendering</p>
    <h1>Counter</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        The classic, and the baseline every other scenario is measured against: one click is one
        <em>onclick → Counter.IncrementCount</em> event and one render of <code>Counter</code>, nothing else.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> The counter</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The second button increments ten times from one event, calling <code>StateHasChanged</code> and awaiting
        between the steps - ten renders that all trace back to a single click.
    </p>

    <div class="bb-demo">
        <div class="bb-demo-bar"><span class="bb-demo-label">Live</span><RendererStatus /></div>

        <p role="status" class="demo-count">Current count: <strong>@currentCount</strong></p>

        <div class="bb-toolbar">
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="IncrementCount">Click me</button>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick="IncrementTenTimes">Increment 10× with awaits (10 renders from one event)</button>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Timeline: one click, one render</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Open the <strong>Timeline</strong> and click <em>Click me</em>: the <em>onclick → Counter.IncrementCount</em>
        event, the one batch it produced and the render of <code>Counter</code> inside it - no layout, header or
        other page component renders with it. The ten-step button shows ten renders caused by the same event, and
        <strong>Diagnostics</strong> calls it a <em>slow event handler</em>: a handler's duration spans every
        <code>await</code> inside it, about 300 ms here.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Profiler: what did this one click cost?</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        In the <strong>Profiler</strong>, press <em>Reset</em>, click once and tick <em>since reset</em>: one row, one
        render. Compare with one keystroke on the <a href="products">Products</a> page.
    </p>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Visit a few pages and come back, then open <em>Renders per page</em> → <em>Show</em>: each page with its visits,
        renders, render time and the three components that cost the most there. This page sits near the bottom; the
        dashboard with its clock ticking sits near the top.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Components: where it is written</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Select <code>Counter</code> in the <strong>Components</strong> tab. <em>Type &amp; source</em> links
        <code>Counter.razor</code>, and <em>Event handlers &amp; callbacks</em> lists both buttons -
        <em>onclick → Counter.IncrementCount</em> and <em>Counter.IncrementTenTimes</em> - each with a link to the line
        that handler is written on. The links open your editor (VS Code by default; <code>Ui.EditorUrlTemplate</code>
        changes it) and come from the app's symbols; without them the location is inferred from the type name and
        labelled so.
    </p>
</div>

@code {
    private int currentCount = 0;

    private void IncrementCount() => currentCount++;

    private async Task IncrementTenTimes()
    {
        for (var i = 0; i < 10; i++)
        {
            currentCount++;
            StateHasChanged();
            await Task.Delay(30);
        }
    }
}
